{
  "case_id": "2016-16-240",
  "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "MICHAEL B. KIMBERLY",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The upshot in practice of the Commonwealth's proposed rule in this case is that when a criminal defendant like Petitioner demonstrates that his trial counsel failed to preserve the fundamental fairness of the criminal proceeding by failing to object and, therefore, allowing to stand a structural error, the gravest kind of constitutional error there can be in the course of a criminal trial. It would be impossible for the defendant to obtain relief under Strickland against Washington for ineffective assistance of counsel. And that's because the Commonwealth says that such -- all such defendants must prove actual prejudice resulting from their attorney's deficiency."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE GINSBURG",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Do you make any distinctions between kinds of errors that we have called structural?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: But, in a way, Mr. Kimberly, I mean, you don't have to convince us of that, do you?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. KIMBERLY",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KIMBERLY: I -- I hope not."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: And what's -- and what -- is there a structural error in this case, in your view, under the hypothetical?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: So the rule that you're asking us to adopt today, Cronic said an absolute absence of counsel, you presume prejudice. We have further said that a conflict of interest, actual conflict of interest, you can presume prejudice. Would an absolute closure, improper closure of a courtroom for no reason, could we presume prejudice there, or would you require the proof of actual prejudice the way you're advocating here?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. RavitZ",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The -- the Court hasn't said it about a reasonable probability standard. It hasn't said that it -- it -- a reasonable probability can't be shown."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. O'Connell",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0002-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I'd like to begin where my co-counsel just left off here with the idea that any time a structural error occurs, it leads to fundamental unfairness and unreliability in the outcome of the trial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is the difficulty in any particular case something that can be weighed in determining whether the defendant has carried his burden of showing prejudice?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What about -- what about counsel's argument we just heard a moment ago, that there's a triviality exception. Might this case qualify for that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Kimberly",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The question there, I think, is whether a public-trial violation happened at all. And although there may be difficult cases where it's unclear whether a closure of a particular length, or the exclusion of some and not all actually amounts to a public-trial violation. After Presley v. Georgia, there's no question --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice BREYER",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Okay. Now, but they -- they want -- and they want to go to the other extreme, basically. But if we're cutting this child in two, what about that as a standard?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But that's the third prong. You're not answering Justice Breyer's question, with respect. That gets you past the third prong, but it doesn't get you home in an unpreserved error case. You still have to meet the fourth prong."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-a885200f5f06-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Doesn't that seem highly unlikely? I mean, a structural error, we think is important, but it isn't automatically a winner every single time if you don't preserve it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2016-16-240",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "a885200f5f06081291f85300686ac4f5331afda76d69a91a1ddc25f85a4d337d",
